    "product_id": "91b24f1a-200b-4b00-ad15-ab5cf698c562",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 220510\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1010 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The overall pattern will favor dry and tranquil conditions across \nthe Desert Southwest through at least the Thanksgiving weekend. \n  \n- Temperatures over the next several days will average a few degrees \nbelow normal before a warming trend pushes readings to above \nseasonal levels by the back half of next week. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nUpper level troughing continues over the Desert SW as the shortwave \ncontinues to push eastwards, and is forecasted to be in the Southern \nPlains by early Sunday morning. As this system passes it will keep \nH5 heights lower, between 576-570 dam, resulting in below normal \ntemperatures today, with highs expected to be in the upper 60's with \nPhoenix having about a 50% chance of staying below 70 degrees. If \nPhoenix does stay below the 70 degree mark it would be the first \ntime since February 20th that a sub-70 high temperatures is seen at \nthe station. \n\nBy Sunday the shortwave will be into the Plain States, as mentioned \nabove, however morning low temperatures will continue to be in the \nmid to upper 40s across the lower deserts and even into the 30s in \nthe higher terrain areas, where a Freeze Warning remains in effect \nfor eastern Gila County Sunday morning. With the passing of the \nshortwave, heights will begin to increase slightly, but will remain \nunder broad troughing. This will lead to afternoon high temperatures \nclimbing back into the low to mid 70s for Sunday, just around normal \nfor late November. By Monday morning another troughing feature, \nalbeit weaker, will quickly pass through Arizona resulting in \ntemperatures cooling 1F-2F, before upper level ridging begins to \nbuild behind the aforementioned trough. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT SATURDAY/...  \nNot much in the way of change can be expected for the longer term \noutlook as the broader flow pattern promotes a continuation of \nbenign conditions. Model guidance points towards a slight rise in \nheights aloft as we progress through the middle portion of the week, \nlikely initiating a gradual warming trend for the region during this \ntimeframe. A greater upward trend in atmospheric heights is shown \ntaking place by Thanksgiving as an unseasonably strong area of high \npressure sets up west of the Baja Peninsula. The good news is, the \nposition of this ridge will put the Desert Southwest under the \ninfluence of cooler northwesterly flow, which will likely prevent \ntemperatures that are completely out of the ordinary for the end of \nNovember. Even though above-normal readings will make a return as \nearly as Wednesday, daily highs during the back half of the week \nshould remain quite pleasant with lower desert readings in the \nmiddle to upper 70s, with perhaps a few spots reaching the lower 80s \nby next weekend. With that being said however, it would not be \nsurprising to see forecasted highs for Thursday and onward trend \nhigher from their current position as the NBM tends to be on the \ncooler side 6-10 days out.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0510Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo weather issues will exist through Sunday night under high cirrus \ndecks. Confidence is very good that light east winds will be \nmaintained through late afternoon before attempting to switch to \nwesterly around sunset. Extended periods of variable directions or \ncalm conditions will be common. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather issues will exist through Sunday night under high cirrus \ndecks. Extended periods of nearly calm conditions will be common \nwith a north component generally favored at KBLH and a west \ntrajectory at KIPL. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry and tranquil conditions will be common across the region through \nthe next several days. Outside of a slight spike on Monday, MinRHs \nvalues will hover between 15-30% through most of the forecast \nperiod, while cool overnight lows will result in decent to good \novernight recovery. Winds overall are expected to be light and favor \ndiurnal trends. Temperatures will hover near to slightly below \nnormal levels through the front half of the upcoming week ahead of \nslight warming later in the forecast period.\n\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...Updated 11/21 1805\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area due to water releases from the dam. Water \nreleases remain around 3000 cfs. Water has now reached the Dateland \narea with a streamflow gauge reporting just under 800 cfs. CBRFC's \nlatest forecast for this gauge has water rising above action stage \n(8 ft) on Sunday. A new Flood Warning segment now extends from \nDateland to Yuma. These water levels will have the potential to \nflood farm fields and low water crossings along the river downstream \nfrom the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Freeze Warning until 9 AM MST Sunday for AZZ563.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...RW/Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero/Benedict\n",
